WEBVTT

00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:01.721
Part six:

00:00:01.771 --> 00:00:05.150
putting the reimagination into practice.

00:00:06.700 --> 00:00:12.370
The journey has clear milestones, and
stakeholders can work on them in parallel.

00:00:12.420 --> 00:00:14.229
Adopt the standard events.

00:00:14.279 --> 00:00:18.806
Bring one exemplar department or scheme
onto iFIX or an adapter.

00:00:18.856 --> 00:00:24.669
Make iFIX the primary channel between the
finance department and line departments.

00:00:24.719 --> 00:00:27.100
Automate utilisation certificates.

00:00:27.150 --> 00:00:31.748
Move accounting and audit onto the
platform and publish accounts.

00:00:31.798 --> 00:00:36.824
Then build new use cases, and use the data
for planning, credit rating,

00:00:36.874 --> 00:00:37.754
and research.

00:00:39.304 --> 00:00:41.418
Five groups of stakeholders.

00:00:41.468 --> 00:00:43.041
The Union government.

00:00:43.091 --> 00:00:43.892
The States.

00:00:43.942 --> 00:00:45.592
Constitutional bodies:

00:00:45.642 --> 00:00:51.157
the Comptroller and Auditor General, the
Controller General of Accounts,

00:00:51.207 --> 00:00:53.321
and the Finance Commissions.

00:00:53.371 --> 00:00:54.713
Local governments.

00:00:54.763 --> 00:00:55.563
And others:

00:00:55.613 --> 00:00:59.582
banks, vendors, software providers, and
researchers.

00:01:01.132 --> 00:01:05.079
The Union Finance Ministry can
institutionalise the standard,

00:01:05.129 --> 00:01:08.749
and use it to harmonise accounting methods
across tiers.

00:01:08.799 --> 00:01:11.108
It can host a central iFIX instance,

00:01:11.158 --> 00:01:16.153
require reporting through APIs rather than
spreadsheets, and reward adopters:

00:01:16.203 --> 00:01:19.626
recognition in the Budget, higher
borrowing ceilings,

00:01:19.676 --> 00:01:23.361
priority disbursal on automated
utilisation certificates.

00:01:23.411 --> 00:01:26.834
Ministries can do the same for the schemes
they fund.

00:01:28.384 --> 00:01:29.897
States are ground zero.

00:01:29.947 --> 00:01:33.364
State finance departments gain the most
visibility.

00:01:33.414 --> 00:01:35.403
They can run a state instance,

00:01:35.453 --> 00:01:39.957
give adopting departments priority
processing and on-time releases,

00:01:40.007 --> 00:01:44.035
share expert support, and present iFIX
data with the budget.

00:01:44.085 --> 00:01:48.317
Line departments, in turn, integrate their
operational systems,

00:01:48.367 --> 00:01:51.851
so that every expenditure links to the
public goods,

00:01:51.901 --> 00:01:54.230
services and benefits it delivered.

00:01:55.780 --> 00:02:01.002
Banks can connect their systems to process
the revenue and expenditure events in

00:02:01.052 --> 00:02:04.034
real time, the foundation for smart
contracts.

00:02:04.084 --> 00:02:08.516
Vendors can submit bills in the standard
format and get paid faster.

00:02:08.566 --> 00:02:13.986
Software vendors and system integrators can
build new applications on the platform,

00:02:14.036 --> 00:02:16.293
contributed to a shared repository.

00:02:16.343 --> 00:02:20.379
And researchers can use the data to
improve fiscal discipline,

00:02:20.429 --> 00:02:25.058
emergency resourcing, debt strategy, and
the inclusiveness of benefits.

00:02:26.608 --> 00:02:27.317
In summary:

00:02:27.367 --> 00:02:31.389
the challenges across India's PFM
landscape share one root,

00:02:31.439 --> 00:02:33.391
fragmented information flows.

00:02:33.441 --> 00:02:37.739
Standardised fiscal and service events,
exchanged through iFIX,

00:02:37.789 --> 00:02:42.846
remove that fragmentation, improve the
timeliness and reliability of data,

00:02:42.896 --> 00:02:46.987
and open the door to reform at every stage
of the lifecycle.

00:02:47.037 --> 00:02:48.782
The way to begin is small:

00:02:48.832 --> 00:02:50.438
one exemplar department.

00:02:52.438 --> 00:02:54.540
That concludes the series.

00:02:54.590 --> 00:02:56.444
Thank you for watching.
